royal advisor."}, {"sourceSlug": "robert-dufford-1st-earl-of-suffolk", "sourceName": "Robert d'Ufford, 1st Earl of Suffolk", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-crecy", "targetName": "Battle of Crécy", "context": "Robert d'Ufford commanded the third division of the English army at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, contributing to one of England's most decisive victories."}, {"sourceSlug": "order-of-the-garter", "sourceName": "Order of the Garter", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "robert-dufford-1st-earl-of-suffolk", "targetName": "Robert d'Ufford, 1st Earl of Suffolk", "context": "Robert d'Ufford was selected as one of the 26 founding members of the Order of the Garter, established by Edward III around 1348, marking him as an elite knight."}], "places": [{"name": "Suffolk, England", "role": "Family Seat & Earldom"}, {"name": "Crécy, France", "role": "Major Battle Site"}, {"name": "Windsor Castle, England", "role": "Order of Garter Founding"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Robert d'Ufford, 1st Earl of Suffolk", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "robert-dufford-1st-earl-of-suffolk"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7351378", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Ufford,_1st_Earl_of_Suffolk"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Robert d'Ufford was a key military and political figure in 14th-century England, playing a crucial role in early English victories in the Hundred Years' War and helping to establish the Order of the Garter. His career exemplifies the loyal aristocratic service that underpinned Edward III's ambitions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-17T08:38:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35199305378", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Robert Ufford, 1st Earl of Suffolk, KG was an English peer. He was created Earl of Suffolk in 1337.", "newValue": "Robert d'Ufford, 1st Earl of Suffolk, KG, emerged as a pivotal English peer and military commander during the tumultuous reign of Edward III, serving as a cornerstone of the English war effort in the early phases of the Hundred Years' War. Born into a prominent East Anglian family, his ascent was marked by unwavering loyalty and distinguished service to the crown. Created Earl of Suffolk in 1337, he solidified his family's position within the English aristocracy and played a crucial role in shaping the military and political landscape of 14th-century England.

Throughout his long career, Ufford was at the forefront of major military engagements, demonstrating tactical acumen and bravery. He campaigned extensively in Scotland, participating in the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333, and later became a key figure in the wars against France. His contributions included significant roles in the naval triumph at the Battle of Sluys in 1340, the innovative chevauchée of 1346 that culminated in the decisive English victory at the Battle of Crécy, and the subsequent Siege of Calais. These campaigns were instrumental in establishing England's early dominance in the Hundred Years' War and defining Edward III's aggressive foreign policy.

Ufford's legacy extends beyond military prowess; he was a statesman who served on numerous royal commissions and councils, influencing domestic policy and administration. His elevation to the earldom marked a significant consolidation of power for the Ufford family in Suffolk, a region vital for its agricultural wealth and strategic coastal access. As one of the original 26 Knights of the Garter, an order established around 1348, he became part of an elite chivalric fraternity that profoundly shaped the ideals of English knighthood and aristocratic identity for centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-17T08:38:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35199305378", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1298-08-16, Thurston"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1298-08-16, Thurston"}], "newValue": ["The English crown's need for loyal and skilled military commanders to prosecute its campaigns in Scotland and France provided opportunities for ambitious aristocrats like Ufford.", "His family's established landed wealth and prominent position in East Anglia provided a strong foundation for his political and military career.", "Edward III's policy of creating new earldoms and elevating loyal supporters strengthened the peerage and rewarded faithful service, directly benefiting Ufford."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-17T08:38:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35199305378", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1369-11-12"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1369-11-12"}], "newValue": ["Robert d'Ufford's military successes contributed significantly to England's early strat